WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.513 --> 00:00:07.877
This brings us to the very
last session of the 2017

00:00:07.878 --> 00:00:08.931
Summer School.

00:00:08.932 --> 00:00:12.620
And this session will
be a little unusual.

00:00:12.621 --> 00:00:14.710
It will not be just me speaking,
but

00:00:14.711 --> 00:00:17.440
we will also have
representatives from the winning

00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:20.890
teams of the Malmo
Collaborative AI Challenge.

00:00:20.891 --> 00:00:24.310
I'm very excited to be able to
hear about their approaches

00:00:24.311 --> 00:00:26.290
in the end of the session.

00:00:26.291 --> 00:00:27.730
But first of all,

00:00:27.731 --> 00:00:31.810
I will give a brief introduction
of the Malmo Platform and

00:00:31.811 --> 00:00:35.400
our long term goals,
why we built this platform.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:38.240
I will introduce the Malmo
Collaborative AI Challenge and

00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:42.130
give you a sense of the task
that teams had to address there.

00:00:42.131 --> 00:00:44.869
And then I'll switch
over to Sean who will

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:49.180
be introducing the teams and who
will discuss their approaches.

00:00:51.938 --> 00:00:55.514
So Project Malmo, you have very
briefly at least heard about

00:00:55.515 --> 00:00:59.301
this yesterday during the dinner
when we presented the awards to

00:00:59.302 --> 00:01:00.395
this team.

00:01:00.396 --> 00:01:03.405
Project Malmo is a platform
that my team and myself have

00:01:03.406 --> 00:01:07.495
developed here in the Cambridge
Lab over the past two years.

00:01:07.496 --> 00:01:10.004
We open sourced this
platform about a year ago.

00:01:10.005 --> 00:01:13.002
So if this is something that
you think is exciting that you

00:01:13.003 --> 00:01:16.186
could potentially use for your
research or use to learn about

00:01:16.187 --> 00:01:19.684
various AI techniques, for
example, reinforcement learning,

00:01:19.685 --> 00:01:22.632
then this is something that
you can download right now.

00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:25.461
It comes with a bunch
of examples that,

00:01:25.462 --> 00:01:28.711
especially Dave there, has put
a lot of time in developing.

00:01:28.712 --> 00:01:32.731
It comes with tutorials, so this
is something ready to try today.

00:01:32.732 --> 00:01:36.587
But why did we feel the need for
developing an experimentation

00:01:36.588 --> 00:01:39.195
platform for
artificial intelligence?

00:01:39.196 --> 00:01:42.699
And why did we select the game
Minecraft as the platform or

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.401
as the basis for
that particular platform?

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.630
The long term aim
of my research and

00:01:49.631 --> 00:01:53.980
a lot of people in
the community is to develop AI

00:01:53.981 --> 00:01:57.457
technology that allows us to
address real-world applications.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.670
Now, when we move towards
real-world applications,

00:02:01.671 --> 00:02:05.120
there's typically a problem
that it's usually expensive,

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:08.885
difficult, or dangerous to
actually do experiments in those

00:02:08.886 --> 00:02:12.130
real-world settings that we're
addressing in the long term.

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:15.405
So there's often this need that
we need to come up with, for

00:02:15.406 --> 00:02:18.290
example, a laboratory
environment or

00:02:18.291 --> 00:02:22.830
a simulated environment in which
we can test out and refine our

00:02:22.831 --> 00:02:26.030
ideas until we have a good
understanding of how they work.

00:02:26.031 --> 00:02:28.283
And we can actually translate
them into real-world

00:02:28.284 --> 00:02:29.760
applications.

00:02:29.761 --> 00:02:32.701
Now you see, computer games,
video games as one

00:02:32.702 --> 00:02:36.223
particularly interesting and
important environment for

00:02:36.224 --> 00:02:38.828
setting up those kinds
of simulations,.

00:02:38.829 --> 00:02:42.127
Because in many important
respects, they are able to

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:45.429
capture some aspects of
real-world interactions,

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.391
while also abstracting from some
of those characteristics that

00:02:49.392 --> 00:02:53.060
make real-world experiments
expensive, dangerous, or

00:02:53.061 --> 00:02:54.185
very difficult.

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:58.050
The reason we selected
Minecraft is that it

00:02:58.051 --> 00:03:01.460
particularly incorporates some
of those characteristics that we

00:03:01.461 --> 00:03:03.890
are particularly interested
in in the research and

00:03:03.891 --> 00:03:07.550
where we also hope to encourage
the research community

00:03:07.551 --> 00:03:09.820
to look at those
particular problems.

00:03:09.821 --> 00:03:12.450
And they're just three of
those aspects that I want to

00:03:12.451 --> 00:03:14.040
mention and emphasize here.

00:03:14.041 --> 00:03:15.670
And there are others as well.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.260
The first aspect is,
that in the real world,

00:03:20.261 --> 00:03:24.210
we have a wide range,
a wide variety of tasks and

00:03:24.211 --> 00:03:27.050
goals that we are trying to
achieve, and that we would like

00:03:27.051 --> 00:03:29.510
AI technologies to
be able to address.

00:03:29.511 --> 00:03:31.960
But at the same time,
those sets of tasks and

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:35.590
problems are typically not
some loose random collection

00:03:35.591 --> 00:03:38.880
of things that we throw at,
for example, people, or

00:03:38.881 --> 00:03:43.380
the AI technologies that we
want to be able to solve them.

00:03:43.381 --> 00:03:45.940
Instead, those problems
are typically related

00:03:45.941 --> 00:03:49.380
according to some rules,
to some you could say,

00:03:49.381 --> 00:03:52.960
common sense regularities that
we find in the real world.

00:03:52.961 --> 00:03:56.130
For example, typically in
real-world environments,

00:03:56.131 --> 00:03:57.540
we have 3D geometry.

00:03:57.541 --> 00:04:00.242
We have certain spacial
regularity that is very

00:04:00.243 --> 00:04:00.992
important.

00:04:00.993 --> 00:04:05.015
And all that information, that
regularity, are things that we

00:04:05.016 --> 00:04:08.900
can put as inductive bias into
the solutions that we develop.

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:10.487
And using those effectively,

00:04:10.488 --> 00:04:13.446
typically allows us
to speed up learning.

00:04:13.447 --> 00:04:16.650
So by developing an
experimentation platform on top

00:04:16.651 --> 00:04:21.870
Minecraft, we are able to use
a world in which there is this

00:04:21.871 --> 00:04:24.420
rich but also regular structure.

00:04:24.421 --> 00:04:27.565
There is a physics in Minecraft
that is not identical to

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:29.240
real-world physics, of course.

00:04:29.241 --> 00:04:32.430
But if you have played Minecraft
before, you know that you cannot

00:04:32.431 --> 00:04:35.890
put water in thin air,
that's sand falls down,

00:04:35.891 --> 00:04:39.910
that when water and lava meet,
they form obsidian, etc., etc.

00:04:39.911 --> 00:04:42.520
So there are all these rules,
all those regularities,

00:04:42.521 --> 00:04:46.560
that can be exploited, and
third upon, when an agent, or

00:04:46.561 --> 00:04:49.820
when an IA Agent, is trying
to learn to solve a new task.

00:04:52.795 --> 00:04:55.502
In addition to that,
what we were looking for

00:04:55.503 --> 00:04:58.993
was an environment that could
support a wide range of ideas

00:04:58.994 --> 00:05:00.077
and approaches.

00:05:00.078 --> 00:05:02.832
And this is something where
Minecraft plays a very

00:05:02.833 --> 00:05:03.977
interesting role.

00:05:03.978 --> 00:05:08.187
For example, in the sense that
it provides a world in which,

00:05:08.188 --> 00:05:11.597
for example, there is a natural
way of discretizing or

00:05:11.598 --> 00:05:16.427
in some characterizing the world
in many different ways.

00:05:16.428 --> 00:05:20.750
And just to give you an example
here, the platform that we have

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:23.760
built does not presuppose
that people use, for example,

00:05:23.761 --> 00:05:25.180
reinforcement learning.

00:05:25.181 --> 00:05:29.090
You can use planning or
other classic AI approaches.

00:05:29.091 --> 00:05:32.019
But just to give you examples
from the reinforcement learning

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.664
literature, this is something
that I'm particularly

00:05:34.665 --> 00:05:35.463
familiar with.

00:05:35.464 --> 00:05:36.885
You can, for example,

00:05:36.886 --> 00:05:40.517
construct tasks that are very
similar to classic discrete,

00:05:40.518 --> 00:05:44.522
state discrete action-types of
reinforcement learning tasks.

00:05:44.523 --> 00:05:49.186
Where, an agent represents
the world, for example, by those

00:05:49.187 --> 00:05:53.146
squares, those individual
innumerable states that

00:05:53.147 --> 00:05:58.370
it can encounter as it explores
the world and interacts with it.

00:05:58.371 --> 00:05:59.480
And at the same time,

00:05:59.481 --> 00:06:01.960
we can move to a different
space of observation.

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:06.240
So for example, the agent could
also observe the video image

00:06:06.241 --> 00:06:08.460
of the world from a first
person perspective.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.830
So we provide a platform where
there are these different

00:06:12.831 --> 00:06:15.750
observation handlers that give
different types of observations

00:06:15.751 --> 00:06:16.710
to the agent.

00:06:16.711 --> 00:06:18.706
There are different kinds
of command handlers.

00:06:18.707 --> 00:06:19.983
And by using those,

00:06:19.984 --> 00:06:23.592
we can create problems that
are specifically targeted at

00:06:23.593 --> 00:06:26.540
the technology that we
are trying to explore.

00:06:26.541 --> 00:06:29.990
And it also allows us to
start combining techniques in

00:06:29.991 --> 00:06:31.080
other ways.

00:06:31.081 --> 00:06:35.030
And I think moving forward,
combining ideas from, for

00:06:35.031 --> 00:06:38.190
example, different communities,
has a lot of potential of

00:06:38.191 --> 00:06:41.520
helping us address interesting
problems in the future.

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.710
Finally, one area that I
am particularly excited

00:06:46.711 --> 00:06:49.774
about is this area of
learning to collaborate and

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:51.880
communicate with other agents.

00:06:51.881 --> 00:06:55.549
And in the long term, the kinds
of agents that I would want

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.930
our AI agents to communicate
with are people.

00:06:58.931 --> 00:07:02.410
So in the long run, we
are aiming to develop technology

00:07:02.411 --> 00:07:05.520
that can collaborate with
people, empower them and

00:07:05.521 --> 00:07:09.610
assist them in achieving the
goals that they are looking for.

00:07:09.611 --> 00:07:14.710
So for those kinds of problems,
creating environments where

00:07:14.711 --> 00:07:16.680
tasks are based on
collaboration and

00:07:16.681 --> 00:07:18.780
communication are very
important.

00:07:18.781 --> 00:07:21.680
The platform that we have
developed comes ready-made for

00:07:21.681 --> 00:07:23.430
those kinds of challenges.

00:07:23.431 --> 00:07:27.159
So for example, at the top here,
there's this screenshot of

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.200
a task where two agents are
collaborating to solve a maze.

00:07:31.201 --> 00:07:33.910
In the lower rung,
there's a very specific example

00:07:33.911 --> 00:07:37.520
of bringing a natural language
component into a task.

00:07:37.521 --> 00:07:40.256
So these are all capabilities
that are all already provided

00:07:40.257 --> 00:07:41.082
by the platform.

00:07:41.083 --> 00:07:45.265
And the platform is very generic
in the way that it allows you to

00:07:45.266 --> 00:07:48.620
create a new task using
a simple XML format.

00:07:48.621 --> 00:07:50.580
Putting on some
reward structure or

00:07:50.581 --> 00:07:54.400
some other information that
should be provided to the agent

00:07:54.401 --> 00:07:58.120
and you're ready and can go
about focusing on experimenting

00:07:58.121 --> 00:08:01.070
with the agent rather than
investing an enormous amount of

00:08:01.071 --> 00:08:03.870
time in setting up some
kind of new simulation or

00:08:03.871 --> 00:08:06.830
some laboratory setting in
which to run the experiments.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.550
Now after this very brief
overview of project Malmo,

00:08:12.551 --> 00:08:16.450
I want to switch to the Malmo
Collaborative AI Challenge.

00:08:16.451 --> 00:08:20.430
And as we mentioned before, the
Malmo Collaborative AI Challenge

00:08:20.431 --> 00:08:23.370
was a challenge that we ran for
the first time this year.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:28.330
And we set this up with
the goal of fostering research

00:08:28.331 --> 00:08:30.800
in especially collaborative AI.

00:08:30.801 --> 00:08:35.194
So in developing, modeling, or
training AI agents that can

00:08:35.195 --> 00:08:37.789
learn to collaborate
with others.

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:43.000
This challenge was open to
participating teams of graduate

00:08:43.001 --> 00:08:44.312
students worldwide.

00:08:44.313 --> 00:08:48.156
And I believe Sean will tell you
more about the participants that

00:08:48.157 --> 00:08:50.020
we actually had.

00:08:50.021 --> 00:08:52.950
It ran in April and
May 2017 of this year.

00:08:52.951 --> 00:08:56.001
And we were able to
award two sets of

00:08:56.002 --> 00:08:58.640
prizes to the winning teams.

00:08:58.641 --> 00:09:01.120
And those decisions
were announced in June,

00:09:01.121 --> 00:09:03.180
just about a month ago.

00:09:03.181 --> 00:09:05.199
There were two sets of prizes,

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.607
one set of prizes
were attendance for

00:09:07.608 --> 00:09:11.270
the winning teams of the AI
Summer School, right here.

00:09:11.271 --> 00:09:14.546
So it's of great pleasure to
welcome those winning teams here

00:09:14.547 --> 00:09:15.750
at the Summer School.

00:09:15.751 --> 00:09:20.178
And the second set of prizes was
an Azure for research grant, and

00:09:20.179 --> 00:09:23.690
like Shemund mentioned
earlier in his talk.

00:09:23.691 --> 00:09:28.180
This is a large amount of
compute resource that we hope

00:09:28.181 --> 00:09:31.940
will allow the winning teams to
take their approaches further,

00:09:31.941 --> 00:09:34.630
invest more in their research,
and come up with novel,

00:09:34.631 --> 00:09:35.890
new ideas and insights.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:40.280
In the remaining few
slides of my talk,

00:09:40.281 --> 00:09:44.630
I want to briefly walk you
through the challenge task and

00:09:44.631 --> 00:09:48.710
to motivate in which way this
is particularly challenging and

00:09:48.711 --> 00:09:52.210
interesting and how it captures
some of the key challenges that

00:09:52.211 --> 00:09:56.420
we need to address in developing
collaborative AI agents.

00:09:56.421 --> 00:09:59.240
And luckily for me, Shemund
has already motivated some of

00:09:59.241 --> 00:10:02.690
the difficulties in
multi-agent scenarios.

00:10:02.691 --> 00:10:07.270
So this goes very much back
to these questions around

00:10:07.271 --> 00:10:09.680
games from a game
theoretic perspective.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:13.380
The task that we
formulated here,

00:10:13.381 --> 00:10:16.420
starts from the classic
formulation, the classic

00:10:16.421 --> 00:10:20.240
game theoretic formulation that
is known as the Stag Hunt.

00:10:20.241 --> 00:10:23.160
And you can see the classic
formulation there.

00:10:23.161 --> 00:10:25.715
This type of game was
first described by

00:10:25.716 --> 00:10:29.190
Jean-Jacques Rousseau
who captured some ideas,

00:10:29.191 --> 00:10:32.410
some kind of challenges
in working out

00:10:32.411 --> 00:10:36.000
social dilemmas around deciding
whether to cooperate and

00:10:36.001 --> 00:10:41.370
coordinate, or whether to go for
your own rewards or safety.

00:10:41.371 --> 00:10:44.300
So the way that this
is typically set up

00:10:44.301 --> 00:10:47.650
is that there are several
hunters who go on a stag hunt.

00:10:47.651 --> 00:10:49.623
If they want to catch a stag,

00:10:49.624 --> 00:10:53.672
they need to collaborate
because it's a very large prey.

00:10:53.673 --> 00:10:56.305
If they manage to
collaborate successfully,

00:10:56.306 --> 00:10:58.180
they all get a high reward.

00:10:58.181 --> 00:11:00.300
So in this example
of two hunters,

00:11:00.301 --> 00:11:03.610
let's say there could
be a value of five to

00:11:03.611 --> 00:11:08.140
everyone who coordinates
in catching the stag.

00:11:08.141 --> 00:11:11.980
Alternatively, each hunter
could try to catch a hare,

00:11:11.981 --> 00:11:15.590
which is easier to catch, can
be caught by an individual, but

00:11:15.591 --> 00:11:17.060
has a smaller payoff.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.010
If everyone catches a hare,
they each get a reward which is

00:11:24.011 --> 00:11:26.800
fairly safe, but it's smaller
than that of coordination.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:32.260
The conflict arises because
if one agent is trying to

00:11:32.261 --> 00:11:35.020
coordinate while the other
is going for the hare,

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:41.010
then the agent that is trying to
coordinate and collaborate will

00:11:41.011 --> 00:11:44.380
actually be empty-handed, so
they will get a reward of zero.

00:11:44.381 --> 00:11:47.390
And so this creates this
tension of trying to estimate

00:11:47.391 --> 00:11:50.390
what the other agent might do.

00:11:50.391 --> 00:11:55.321
There are two pure Nash
equilibriums here in

00:11:55.322 --> 00:11:57.950
this make sum game.

00:11:57.951 --> 00:11:59.480
One is where both coordinate.

00:11:59.481 --> 00:12:01.852
One is where both go for
the hare.

00:12:01.853 --> 00:12:02.594
And then,

00:12:02.595 --> 00:12:07.053
there's also a mixed strategy
that uses various percentages,

00:12:07.054 --> 00:12:10.200
various probabilities for
one or the other.

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.862
Now, the variant of this team
that we based our task on,

00:12:14.863 --> 00:12:18.682
was this extended version of the
stag hunt that was proposed by

00:12:18.683 --> 00:12:21.050
Yoshida and colleagues in 2008.

00:12:21.051 --> 00:12:23.831
And I particularly
like this form of

00:12:23.832 --> 00:12:28.744
the game because it extends it
with some various aspects around

00:12:28.745 --> 00:12:33.016
understanding how to
coordinate with another agent.

00:12:33.017 --> 00:12:36.429
One aspect that they were
particularly interested in in

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:39.700
studying is what they
call the Theory of Mind.

00:12:39.701 --> 00:12:43.110
And this is this idea
that if you want to

00:12:43.111 --> 00:12:47.600
compute your optimal strategy,
then you need to know

00:12:47.601 --> 00:12:51.560
some information about the other
agent's information state.

00:12:51.561 --> 00:12:57.360
So you need to reason about
the other's agent's strategy

00:12:57.361 --> 00:13:00.820
in order to conjure with an
effective strategy of your own.

00:13:00.821 --> 00:13:03.995
And this creates, again, this
recursive dilemma of reasoning

00:13:03.996 --> 00:13:07.070
about what they, what you know,
what they know, etc, etc.

00:13:07.071 --> 00:13:09.705
Also, this formulation
introduces and

00:13:09.706 --> 00:13:12.030
interesting aspect of signaling,

00:13:12.031 --> 00:13:15.510
which you could see as
some kind of language.

00:13:15.511 --> 00:13:20.670
So for example, if I'm the green
agent down here at the bottom,

00:13:20.671 --> 00:13:24.520
and I move let's say to
the right, then the red

00:13:24.521 --> 00:13:27.880
agent could infer that as
a signal that you were trying to

00:13:27.881 --> 00:13:31.490
move towards the stag, that you
are trying to be cooperative.

00:13:31.491 --> 00:13:34.170
So then you can have this
additional dimension where you

00:13:34.171 --> 00:13:36.730
try to interpret the other
agent's actions.

00:13:36.731 --> 00:13:39.970
In a way that allows you to gain
additional information about

00:13:39.971 --> 00:13:41.030
their strategy.

00:13:41.031 --> 00:13:43.670
So it's an interesting
form of communication

00:13:43.671 --> 00:13:46.190
that doesn't require
natural language per se.

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:51.680
Now our formulation of the task
was a translation of this game

00:13:51.681 --> 00:13:55.300
theoretics stag hunt problem
into the Minecraft world.

00:13:55.301 --> 00:13:58.300
You can see here the interface
that we provide for

00:13:58.301 --> 00:14:00.320
human players to play the game.

00:14:00.321 --> 00:14:03.500
So, you can actually try this,
and try this out again.

00:14:03.501 --> 00:14:06.529
This is something where
we provided the task and

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:09.410
the baseline starter
code on GitHub.

00:14:09.411 --> 00:14:12.346
So if you would like to play
around with those kinds of

00:14:12.347 --> 00:14:14.896
tasks, you can download this and
try it out.

00:14:14.897 --> 00:14:17.581
We provide two kinds of
observations to the human

00:14:17.582 --> 00:14:18.110
player.

00:14:18.111 --> 00:14:22.941
And for the AI agents, teams
are also free to choose one or

00:14:22.942 --> 00:14:23.980
the other.

00:14:23.981 --> 00:14:27.490
The first one is
a first-person perspective,

00:14:27.491 --> 00:14:31.040
which creates this very
difficult problem of having

00:14:31.041 --> 00:14:33.490
to deal with partial
observability.

00:14:33.491 --> 00:14:37.007
The other one is a more global
observation based on your

00:14:37.008 --> 00:14:39.310
top down view of
the current agent.

00:14:39.311 --> 00:14:41.860
So here,
I would be the red agent.

00:14:41.861 --> 00:14:44.441
I could see the pig and
the blue agent position there.

00:14:44.442 --> 00:14:48.702
And then, my goal would be to
pick a strategy that allows

00:14:48.703 --> 00:14:52.885
me to either catch the pig
by cornering or pinching it.

00:14:52.886 --> 00:14:54.644
Or, I could go for safety,

00:14:54.645 --> 00:14:58.467
go to one of the exit squares on
the left or to the right and get

00:14:58.468 --> 00:15:02.390
the small reward by abandoning
my potential collaborator.

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:07.340
The task was set up in
a turn taking fashion so

00:15:07.341 --> 00:15:11.070
each agent would act
one after the other.

00:15:11.071 --> 00:15:13.960
However, the pig was maintained
with normal Minecraft dynamics

00:15:13.961 --> 00:15:16.650
so it could suddenly
start to run around.

00:15:16.651 --> 00:15:20.256
And there might be strategies,
for example, in waiting in

00:15:20.257 --> 00:15:23.660
a strategic position until
the pig might show up there.

00:15:23.661 --> 00:15:26.540
I don't want to say much
more about the game and

00:15:26.541 --> 00:15:27.760
how it was solved.

00:15:27.761 --> 00:15:30.140
We will hear about that,
as I mentioned,

00:15:30.141 --> 00:15:32.861
from the winning teams,
in just a few minutes.

00:15:32.862 --> 00:15:36.720
One thing that impressed me
personally a lot was to see

00:15:36.721 --> 00:15:39.208
the huge variety
of approaches and

00:15:39.209 --> 00:15:44.680
strategies that teams tried out
for this particular challenge.

00:15:44.681 --> 00:15:46.856
And our conclusion, so far,

00:15:46.857 --> 00:15:50.430
was that there was no
obvious winning strategy.

00:15:50.431 --> 00:15:54.279
And I think, moving forward,
it will be really interesting to

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:57.693
think about the various
ingredients that make people be

00:15:57.694 --> 00:16:00.100
successful in this
particular task.

00:16:00.101 --> 00:16:03.050
And to understand how some of
those might be combined into

00:16:03.051 --> 00:16:05.141
future, more effective
strategies for

00:16:05.142 --> 00:16:06.811
solving collaborative games.

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:14.454
I just want to conclude my
slides with a short outlook.

00:16:14.455 --> 00:16:18.971
I think Collaborative AI, so
developing AI technology that

00:16:18.972 --> 00:16:23.844
can learn how to collaborate,
especially with humans, has huge

00:16:23.845 --> 00:16:28.631
potential for transforming the
way we currently think about how

00:16:28.632 --> 00:16:32.990
we interact with machines and
what machines can do for us.

00:16:32.991 --> 00:16:36.101
I think this is an immensely
rewarding research area.

00:16:36.102 --> 00:16:39.154
And you happen to be in
a position that is perfect for

00:16:39.155 --> 00:16:43.130
developing the next generation
of collaborative AI technology.

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.552
I'd like to encourage you to
think about those problems.

00:16:46.553 --> 00:16:49.943
And if there are any areas that
you're particularly interested

00:16:49.944 --> 00:16:52.703
in, for example, in terms
of creating new tasks for

00:16:52.704 --> 00:16:54.776
future collaborative
AI challenges,

00:16:54.777 --> 00:16:56.755
then come talk to
me after this talk.

00:16:56.756 --> 00:16:58.818
Thank you so much.

00:16:58.819 --> 00:17:01.303
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

